speechtext:
Hon. J. D. Reid (Minister of Railways) moved for leave to introduce Bill No. 93 (from the Senate), to amend the Railway Act (Location of Railways).
Motion agreed to and Bill read the first time.
THE DOMINION IRON AND STEEL;

While the subject is under discussion, I feel that I must say a few words, because I am in rather a peculiar position. Of all the subjects that have been under discussion this session, I think no particular one has brought more grist to the legislative mill than the question of total prohibition. I have been, I think, bombarded by every W.C.T.U. in the country-at least in that portion of it from which I come-and, in addition, representations have come to me from all the

churches in my constituency, and from a trade protection society. I quite agree with the minister that there is a very large temperance class which must be considered by the Government in dealing with this question. On the other hand, Mr. Chairman, I have had expressed from the labour organizations the request that this Government should come out boldly and declare for the consumption of beer and wine. Furthermore, I have been brought into contact with the man in the street, sometimes called the " silent voter He has said to me, " Major, we did not vote for total prohibition, we voted for the Macdonald Act " -I am referring to legislation enacted in my own province-" and I should like another vote. We submitted to this total prohibition as a war measure, realizing that every grain of barley counted. We were willing to submit to that, but now that the war is over we want to see that legislation wiped out That is a very considerable vote in my constituency, too, Sir. The point has been brought to my 5 p.m. attention that this is class legislation between the rich and the poor, but that is not an argument. I never heard it advanced as an argument, except as to how it affects individuals; some fellows have the good luck to have their cellars full of liquor while the cellars of others are very, very empty. I felt that I had to say something on this question, because in my constituency there are a very large number of good temperance people. Personally, I stand absolutely and entirely four-square against total prohibition, and I shall try and give my reasons for occupying that position.
First of all, as to the medical side of this question. A committee appointed in England by the Central Control Board to inquire into the liquor traffic has recently completed its work, and the report of that committee bearing the imprint " H. M. Stationery Office, 1918 " is very interesting. The committee in question consisted of the following gentlemen:
Lord D'Abernon, G.C.M.G. (chairman), chairman of the Central Control Board (liquor traffic) ;
Sir George Newman, K.C.B., M.D., (vice-chairman), Principal Medical Officer to the Board of Education, and member of the Central Control Board (liquor traffic) ;
Prof. A. R. Cushny, M.D., F.R.S., Professor of Pharmacology at University College, London ;
H. H. Dale, M.D., P.R.S., Head of the Department of Bio-chemistry and Pharmacology under the Medical Research Committee, National Health Insurance;

M. Greenwood, M.R.C.S., Statistician to the Lister Institute of Preventive Medicine, and Reader in Medical Statistics in the University of London;
W. McDougall, M.B., P.R.S., Reader in Mental Philosophy in the University of Oxford, and Fellow of Corpus Christi College, Oxford;
F. W. Mott, M.D., F.R.S., Pathologist to the London County Asylums, and Consulting Physician to Charing Cross Hospital;
Prof. C. S. Sherrington, M.D., F.R.S., Wayn-flete Professor of Physiology in the University of Oxford, and Fellow of Magdalen College, Oxford;
W. C. Sullivan, M.D., Medical Superintendent of the Rampton State Asylum for Criminal Lunatics.
It will be seen that the committee was composed of very eminent men, whose reputation .is world-wide, and to ignore their findings is to ignore the conclusions of scientists-you might as well pull down your colleges and be done with it. With your permission, Mr. Chairman, I will read a few short extracts from the report. The first is as follows:
Apart from the results of its continued excessive use, the main effects of alcohol that have any real significance are due to its action
on the nervous system	The result of
scientific research concerning the action of alcohol on the respiration, the circulation, the digestion, the muscular system, is to show that, so far as direct action is concerned, alcohol, when administered in moderate doses, in dilute form, and at sufficient intervals, has no effect of any serious and practical account, (p. 125).
Again the report says:
A further conclusion of capital importance which emerges with equal clearness, is that the action of alcohol on the nervous system is essentially sedative, and-with the possible exception of its direct influence on the respiratory centre-it is not truly stimulant, (p. 125.)
Hon. gentlemen will note that the committee consider the action of alcohol on the nervous system is essentially sedative, and if there ever was a time in our history when the frazzled nerves of a tormented world needed a sedative, it is now.
